        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Towers</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these towers. They were chosen from listings, maker product pages,
            recall records and independent coverage, and the claims on this page are limited to what
            those sources support.
          </p>
          <p>
            We began with the towers that come up most often for this search on Amazon and in
            independent coverage — Sportsroyals, RELIFE, Marcy, Stamina, Body Champ, <a href="/bowflex/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>BowFlex</a>,
            Fitness Reality and a long tail of marketplace brands — and kept only those we could
            confirm as on sale on 19 October 2026 and whose key figures appeared in a second source.
            The independent sources were FitnessVolt's hands-on review of the Sportsroyals tower
            (August 2026), its roundups of power towers and pull-up stands (updated May and
            September 2026), and SET FOR SET's power tower comparison (June 2025).
          </p>
          <p>
            That process removed more towers than it kept. BowFlex's BodyTower appears in older
            roundups, but BowFlex's own store listed no BodyTower on 19 October, so we treated it as
            no longer sold. Marcy's SteelBody STB-98501, a well-regarded heavier tower, is marked
            retired on Marcy's store. Stamina's X Power Tower with VKR and Marcy's TC-5580 are on
            sale, but we found their ratings and dimensions only on the makers' own pages. Fitness
            Reality's X-Class, Body Champ's VKR1010 and several marketplace towers appear in
            roundups, but we could not find a maker specification to check the roundups against.
            Body-Solid sells gym-grade knee-raise and dip stations on its own store, but the pages
            we read did not state a user limit and several showed contradictory stock messages.
            Those are mentioned here rather than carded.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every brand was checked against the CPSC recall database by recall title,
            manufacturer and recall description, and the category was searched for pull-up, chin-up
            and dip products. We found no recalls for Sportsroyals or RELIFE. Marcy's importer,
            Impex, recalled Olympic dumbbell handles in 2021, not a tower. Stamina's recalls on record
            are for an exercise machine in 1996, mini-trampolines in 2006, <a href="/best-inversion-tables/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>inversion tables</a> in 2008
            and ellipticals in 2009, none of them a power tower. In the category, the database lists
            the Nautilus F3 Chin Dip station sold to fitness centres (2009, footpads that could
            break), TRX dip and hammer bars (2014, welds that could crack), the Perfect Pullup doorway
            bar (2011, handles that could crack) and an Ethos pull-up assist band (2019, a clip that
            could break). None is a current model on this page, but the failures they describe —
            welds, feet and plastic parts — are the ones to inspect on any tower, new or used.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="capacity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Weight Capacity: Listing Claims vs What You Can Verify</h2>
<p>
            Weight capacity is the most prominent number on almost every power tower listing and the
            hardest to trust. Three things we found while checking show why.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The same name, three ratings.</strong> The Sportsroyals Power Tower we carded is
            listed at 360 lb, and FitnessVolt's reviewer, who assembled that exact listing, says so.
            FitnessVolt's own roundup lists a Sportsroyals Power Tower at 450 lb with a different bar
            height range and six height settings instead of eight, and SET FOR SET's table lists one
            at 400 lb. These are probably different versions sold under very similar titles, which
            is exactly the problem: a review of one version can quote the rating of another. RELIFE's
            tower has the same pattern. RELIFE's store says 400 lb, SET FOR SET listed 330 lb in 2025,
            and FitnessVolt reports a Walmart listing that gives 450 lb in its specification fields
            while its text says 400.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Frame weight and rating do not track each other.</strong> RELIFE lists its tower
            at a net weight of 41.4 lb with a 400 lb rating. Stamina lists its Full Body Power Tower
            735 at 80 lb with a 300 lb rating on the tower stations, and its Power Tower 1698 at 54 lb
            with 250 lb. A heavier frame is not automatically stronger, but a light frame with a high
            rating should make you ask what the rating describes. Listings rarely say whether the
            figure is a static load hung on the bar, a tested dynamic load, or simply the heaviest
            user the seller expects; none of the listings we read named a test standard.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What to do with the number.</strong> Treat it as a ceiling, not a target. Count
            everything on the tower — your body weight, a <a href="/best-weighted-vests-for-walking/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>weighted vest</a> or dip belt, and the extra
            force of dropping into the bottom of a dip — and stay well inside the lowest rating you
            have seen for that model. If you are close to the limit, or plan weighted pull-ups and
            dips, choose a tower from a maker whose figure two sources agree on, or move up to a
            bolted rack. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill weight capacity</a>
            explains why a margin below any stated limit is sensible on home equipment generally,
            and the same reasoning applies here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stability" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Stability, Tipping and Anchoring</h2>
<p>
            A power tower is a tall frame on a footprint about the size of a doormat, loaded at the
            top. Most of the wobble owners complain about comes from that geometry, from assembly, or
            from the floor underneath, and most of it can be reduced.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Base size against bar height.</strong> The further the feet extend in the
            direction you swing, the harder the tower is to rock. The towers here range from RELIFE's
            compact base, about 35 by 26.4 inches, to Stamina's 735 at 75 by 39.5 inches with its
            bench. A compact base is easier to fit and more likely to move under a swinging rep.
            FitnessVolt's reviewer describes the Sportsroyals' broad H-shaped base as spreading the
            load and sitting flat, but says it does not eliminate movement.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>None of these is designed to be bolted down.</strong> The listings and maker pages
            we read describe freestanding use, sometimes with suction-cup or anti-slip feet, and give
            no anchoring instructions. That means two things. First, train strictly: kipping,
            butterfly pull-ups, muscle-ups and swinging leg raises put sideways and lifting forces on
            the frame that controlled reps do not, and FitnessVolt names kipping as the movement that
            exposes the wobble. Second, do not improvise anchors by drilling through feet or tying the
            frame to a wall unless the maker says you may; you can void the warranty and create a
            weaker point than the one you meant to fix.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Face the right way on dips and knee raises.</strong> Use the dip handles and the
            knee-raise pads from the side the maker shows in the manual. On most towers that keeps
            your weight inside the base. Leaning out over the open side, or using the frame as an
            anchor for bands or rings pulled sideways, is how a light tower tips.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The floor matters as much as the frame.</strong> Stand the tower on a hard, level
            surface. FitnessVolt recommends dense gym flooring over soft interlocking tiles, which can
            let the feet sink unevenly and rock. Rubber stall mats are a good base; our
            <a href="/best-gym-flooring/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">gym flooring guide</a>
            compares the options. On carpet, put a rigid board or mat under the whole footprint. Keep
            a clear zone around the tower, away from doors, glass, a parked car in a garage, or a
            loaded barbell.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Tighten, then tighten again.</strong> A frame that rocks on a level floor is
            usually out of square from assembly. Re-square it, then recheck every bolt after the
            first few sessions, because new frames settle. That one habit removes more wobble than
            any accessory.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="height" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Bar Height for Tall Users and Low Ceilings</h2>
<p>
            Height is where a power tower most often disappoints. Tall users need a bar high enough to
            hang from with legs clear of the floor, and the room needs space above the bar for the top
            of every rep. The figures for the towers here:
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Tower</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Height figure</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Source</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sportsroyals (360 lb listing)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Bar 57.1 to 83.1 in., 8 settings</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">FitnessVolt review of the listing</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">RELIFE</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 58.5 to 89 in., 9 settings</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">RELIFE and SET FOR SET agree</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Marcy TC-3515</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">89 in. maximum frame height</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Marcy and FitnessVolt agree</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stamina 735</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">84 or 85 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">SET FOR SET and Stamina differ by an inch</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stamina 1698</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not confirmed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stamina's page only</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>Frame height is not bar height.</strong> On most towers the pull-up bar is the top
            of the frame, but grips can sit slightly lower or angle forward, and Marcy publishes a
            maximum frame height rather than a bar height. Where only the frame height is given,
            assume the bar is at or a little below it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Will you hang straight?</strong> Stand under where the bar would be, reach both
            arms straight up and measure from the floor to your fingertips. If the top bar setting is
            below that, you will pull with bent knees on every rep. That works — plenty of people
            train that way — but it feels less natural, and FitnessVolt lists very tall athletes as a
            poor fit for the Sportsroyals for that reason. Taller users should look at the two
            towers that reach 89 inches, or at a rack or wall-mounted bar, which can be mounted
            higher.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Will it fit under the ceiling?</strong> At the top of a pull-up your chin clears
            the bar, so the top of your head rises above it by roughly the distance from your chin to
            the crown of your head. Measure that, add a few inches of margin, and add it to the bar
            height you plan to use. Then subtract the thickness of any flooring. In a basement with
            a low ceiling, the adjustable RELIFE and Sportsroyals let you lower the bar to fit; the
            fixed towers do not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Short users and children</strong> need the opposite: a low setting for dips and
            knee raises. RELIFE says its lowest setting is designed for children, and adjustable
            towers generally suit households with very different heights better than fixed ones.
            Supervise children on any tower; the frame is not a climbing toy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="assembly" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Assembly and Setup</h2>
<p>
            Power towers are among the easier home gym assemblies, but how carefully you build one
            decides how steady it feels. We have not assembled these towers; this is what the makers
            and the reviewer say, and the habits that apply to any tower.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Use two people.</strong> Uprights are long and awkward to hold square while
            the first bolts go in. Marcy ships its towers by FedEx in one box; RELIFE gives a package
            size of about 54.5 by 18.7 by 4.3 inches and a gross weight of 44.5 lb.</li>
            <li><strong>Build loose, then tighten.</strong> FitnessVolt's reviewer advises starting
            every bolt loosely, squaring the frame on a flat surface, and tightening in stages. Fully
            tightening one joint first can lock the frame out of square, and a frame out of square
            rocks.</li>
            <li><strong>Use the lock nuts.</strong> RELIFE tells owners of its PT06 to tighten the
            lock nuts firmly, because they stop nuts working loose under vibration. Do not swap them
            for plain nuts.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the adjusters.</strong> On height-adjustable towers, the pins or knobs
            carry your full weight. Make sure each one passes fully through both tubes before you
            hang. RELIFE sells replacement knobs and pads for its towers on its store, which is
            useful to know before one goes missing.</li>
            <li><strong>Inspect the welds on arrival.</strong> Look along every weld for cracks or
            gaps, and photograph any damage before assembly. A cracked weld is a return, not a
            repair.</li>
            <li><strong>Recheck after the first week.</strong> Frames settle as pads compress and
            bolts seat. Retighten everything after the first few sessions, and then monthly.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our guide to
            <a href="/best-home-gym-accessories/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home gym accessories</a>
            covers the small tools and storage that make maintenance easier.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tower-vs-bar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Power Tower vs Pull-Up Bar vs Rack</h2>
<p>
            A power tower is one of three ways to get a pull-up bar into a home gym, and it is not
            always the right one.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Doorway and wall-mounted bars</h3>
          <p>
            A doorway bar costs little and stores away; a wall- or ceiling-mounted bar, fixed into
            studs or joists, is the steadiest option short of a rack and can be mounted at any
            height. Neither gives you dips or knee raises, and both depend on the door frame or the
            structure you fix them to. Our
            <a href="/best-pull-up-bars/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">pull-up bar guide</a>
            covers them in detail.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Power tower</h3>
          <p>
            Freestanding, so there is no drilling, and it adds dips, knee raises and push-ups. The
            costs are floor space, a frame that can move, and a bar height set by the tower rather
            than by you. It is the right choice if you want the extra stations, rent, or cannot fix
            anything to walls or ceilings.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Rack or all-in-one machine</h3>
          <p>
            A power rack with a pull-up bar, especially one bolted to the floor, is steadier than any
            tower and lets you squat and press as well; many racks take dip attachments. It costs and
            weighs far more. An all-in-one home gym machine gives you assisted pulling through a lat
            pulldown rather than true pull-ups. Our guides to
            <a href="/best-squat-racks-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">squat racks</a>
            and
            <a href="/best-home-gym-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home gym machines</a>
            cover those routes.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you cannot yet do a pull-up, a tower is still useful: dips, knee raises and
            hanging holds build towards it, and a looped band hung from the bar takes some of your
            weight. Our
            <a href="/best-resistance-bands/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">resistance band guide</a>
            covers pull-up assistance bands. Check the band's clips and loops for wear, which was the
            failure in the 2019 pull-up assist recall.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safe-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Using a Power Tower Safely</h2>
<p>
            Most power tower injuries come from falls and from shoulders pushed too far, not from
            frames breaking. A few habits cover most of the risk.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Treat the rating as the maker's figure.</strong> Stay well inside the lowest
            rating you have seen for your model, counting any vest or dip belt.</li>
            <li><strong>Control every rep.</strong> No kipping or swinging on a freestanding tower,
            and lower into dips under control rather than dropping into the bottom.</li>
            <li><strong>Step on and off deliberately.</strong> Use the push-up handles or a stable
            step to reach the bar, not the knee-raise pads at an angle.</li>
            <li><strong>Check before each session.</strong> Look for loose bolts, shifted feet, a pin
            not fully home, and splits in the grips or pads.</li>
            <li><strong>Mind the dip depth.</strong> Deep dips load the front of the shoulder heavily.
            How deep is right for you is a question for a coach or physiotherapist, not a listing.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep children off it unsupervised.</strong> A child climbing the side of a
            light tower can tip it.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you are combining bodyweight work with running or walking, our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-before-or-after-weights/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">doing the treadmill before or after weights</a>
            covers the order of a combined session.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If you are near or above any tower's rating</strong>, or want weighted pull-ups
            and dips, a bolted rack or a wall-mounted bar fixed into structure is the safer route.
            Our
            <a href="/best-squat-racks-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">squat rack guide</a>
            covers racks with pull-up bars and dip attachments.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you only want pull-ups</strong>, a
            <a href="/best-pull-up-bars/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">pull-up bar</a>
            costs less and takes no floor space.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want a full strength setup in one frame</strong>, an all-in-one machine
            gives presses, pulldowns, leg work and cables; see our guide to
            <a href="/best-home-gym-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home gym machines</a>.
            For loaded upper-body work without a machine, a
            <a href="/best-weight-benches/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight bench</a>
            and
            <a href="/best-adjustable-dumbbells/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">adjustable dumbbells</a>
            pair well with a tower.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you have a shoulder or elbow problem</strong>, pull-ups and dips are among the
            hardest exercises on those joints. Get advice before you buy, and consider starting with
            a cable machine, where the load can be set below your body weight; our
            <a href="/best-cable-machines-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cable machine guide</a>
            covers the options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best power tower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people on a budget, the Sportsroyals Power Tower, which an independent reviewer assembled and found sat flat on its broad base, though not without movement. The RELIFE suits low ceilings and families because its bar adjusts lowest, and the Marcy TC-3515 is the choice from an established brand whose 300 lb rating two sources agree on.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are power tower weight ratings accurate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Treat them as seller figures. We found the same tower name sold with ratings of 360, 400 and 450 lb, and another tower listed at 330, 400 and 450 lb in different places. Listings rarely say how the rating was set. Stay well inside the lowest figure you see for your model, and count any weighted vest or dip belt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop a power tower from wobbling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Assemble it with bolts loose, square the frame on a level floor, then tighten in stages and recheck after the first few sessions. Stand it on dense rubber flooring rather than soft tiles or carpet, use the dip and knee-raise stations from the side the manual shows, and train without kipping or swinging, which is what exposes most wobble.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a power tower good for tall people?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the top bar setting is above your straight-arm overhead reach. Measure from the floor to your fingertips with arms raised and compare it with the bar height, not the frame height. Of the towers here, the RELIFE and Marcy TC-3515 reach about 89 inches; the Sportsroyals tops out at about 83. Check the ceiling too, allowing for your head above the bar.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you bolt a power tower to the floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The towers on this page are designed as freestanding and their makers give no anchoring instructions. Do not drill through the feet or tie the frame to a wall unless the maker says you may, as that can void the warranty. If you need a frame that will not move, a bolted power rack or a wall-mounted pull-up bar is the better tool.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Power tower or pull-up bar: which should I buy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Buy a pull-up bar if you only want pull-ups and can fix one to a door frame, wall or ceiling; it is cheaper, steadier and takes no floor space. Buy a power tower if you also want dips, knee raises and push-ups, rent, or cannot drill. A power rack with a pull-up bar beats both if you also want to lift a barbell.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Power Tower</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these is easy to avoid before you order and frustrating to discover once the tower is built.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the rating in the title</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Power towers are sold in versions with different ratings under near-identical names, and reviews quote whichever version they saw. Open the listing you are buying, find the rating in the specification table, compare it with the manual if you can, and stay well inside the lowest figure you have seen.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Checking the frame height but not your reach</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A tower that looks tall online can still leave a tall user pulling with bent knees. Measure your straight-arm overhead reach and compare it with the top bar setting, then add head clearance above the bar and check the ceiling at the exact spot, minus any flooring.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Kipping on a freestanding frame</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Swinging pull-ups, muscle-ups and kipping put sideways and lifting forces on a frame that is not bolted to anything, and that is when towers rock and walk across the floor. Keep reps strict on any tower, and move to a bolted rack or a structural bar if dynamic work is part of your training.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never retightening the bolts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">New frames settle as bolts seat and pads compress, and a tower that felt solid on day one can rock by week two. Retighten every bolt after the first few sessions and then monthly, and check that height pins are fully home before each workout.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

